In a buy-back programme for remanufacturing, the manufacturer offers customers a financial incentive to return used products. The returned products (called "cores") are then assessed, disassembled, cleaned, and remanufactured to original specifications. The designer plays a critical role in ensuring products are designed for efficient core recovery — this means using durable materials for structural components, designing for non-destructive disassembly, and minimising the use of adhesives or irreversible joints that would damage components during recovery.